{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And the multitude of his sons</b> The grammarians said that its meaning is the greatness of his sons, like קִרְיַת מֶלֶךְ רָב (a great royal city), for how would he tell his wife that he has many sons? In my opinion, he thus recounted to his friends, along with his wife, his good fortune, that he has great wealth and many sons, and none of this is worth anything in his eyes, even though the king has promoted him, and in his great prominence he drank with the queen and will drink again tomorrow. And the sense of 'sits at the king's gate' is because it is a great distinction, and he provokes me."], :book :esther, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Kol Sason, Krotoschin, 1840" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001728315"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:03.686271868Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}